message.content:
You work in the repo of team error404.ai for the Atira EHL Challenge ("From RFQ to bid").

Read these first:
- CLAUDE.md
- the full rules: `curl -s https://ehl-challenge.atira.ai/challenges/rfq.md`
- the new evals docs: `curl -s https://ehl-challenge.atira.ai/docs/evals.md`, also at /Users/jonasstenglein/.claude/jobs/064ce75e/tmp/evals.md
- `atira eval --help`
- docs/plan.md (public-1 scouting notes)

Follow all rules:
- git: author `josteng <jonas@stenglein.dev>`, imperative subject <= 50 chars, `-` bullet body, no Co-Authored-By or session links, never stage `.claude/`, never push;
- run `atira ... </dev/null` with timeouts >= 120 s;
- kill processes by PID only;
- scope: only your own worktree and branch; never touch `codex/*` branches or `/private/tmp` worktrees;
- never copy real values from the challenge sites into the eval; invent all data.

Why: the final ranking is a run on a PRIVATE dataset. The organizers say most teams lost quality there through small things with large effects. An eval lets us test our agent (`agent/`, main) on our own world.

Goal: build two private evals that exercise the rule traps public-1 never triggers. Keep the site structure close to the starter kit and public-1, so the traps, not the layout, are what is tested; another agent covers layout.

1. **"err404-nobid"**: a scenario whose correct answer is `no_bid`. One position has no exactly matching active material: its only candidate is inactive, OR the match exists only before a scoped amendment that the Q&A makes. The ERP shows look-alike materials, and a "nutzbarer Weg" (rule 20) case nearly matches. Also add:
   - a conditional discount approval (rule 16; it gives 0 % for no_bid);
   - a superseded approval;
   - a future-dated authority mail.

   `expected.json` must follow the rules for no_bid exactly: no positions, total 0, discount per rule.
2. **"err404-traps"**: a bid scenario with:
   - a conditional discount whose threshold FAILS (undiscounted scale prices, inclusive), while an older unconditional approval exists that must NOT revive;
   - a withdrawn position;
   - a quantity change;
   - authority mails from the wrong sender or domain, and look-alike tender references;
   - a future-dated record;
   - a delivery commitment whose pooled demand is just above the inclusive ceiling;
   - stock pooled per material across two positions (rule 15);
   - a price scale boundary hit exactly;
   - a metal index window boundary on the scenario date;
   - HALF_UP rounding cases at .005.

   Derive `expected.json` by hand, carefully, with a derivation note per number.

Where: put each eval in `evals/<name>/` in your worktree; commit them on your branch. They are outside `agent/`, so they are not part of the package.

Steps:
1. Start each eval with `atira eval init` and keep it small (plain Node).
2. Test locally with `atira eval dev`. Another agent builds an eval at the same time: check `atira eval dev --help` for a port option and use a non-default range if one exists; otherwise rely on `deploy` + `preview`.
3. Check that a hand-entered correct answer scores 100 (via `preview`).
4. Run our agent: local runs against `eval dev` are fine (`eval "$(atira eval dev --env)"`, then `(cd agent && HEADLESS=1 uv run --no-sync python agent.py)`). The model proxy is already running on 127.0.0.1:8765, so do not start another.
5. `atira eval deploy` (private).
6. `atira eval run --package ./<export> --repeats 1` with main's `agent/`: export it with `git -C /Users/jonasstenglein/OtherProjects/EHL_Finale/error404ai_ehl_finals archive main agent` into a temp dir under `$CLAUDE_JOB_DIR/tmp`. Also run `--package submission` once per eval. The 12-runs-per-hour limit is shared with the other eval agent: use at most 4 eval runs in total.

Report:
- eval ids and slugs, and the traps in each;
- the scores of main and of the submission, with breakdowns;
- for every lost point: the concrete agent failure, located in our code (file:line) where possible, with a suggested fix.

Do NOT change `agent/`; only report.
